NAME
       blkid - show the type, label and UUID of block devices

SYNOPSIS
       blkid [-o FORMAT] [-s TAG]... [DEVICE...]
       blkid -L LABEL
       blkid -U UUID
       blkid -p DEVICE

DESCRIPTION
       blkid reads the start of each block device (disk, partition, logical
       volume) and reports what is written there: the file system or swap
       TYPE, its UUID and LABEL, and for partitions the PARTUUID (and on GPT
       disks the PARTLABEL). One line per device:

           /dev/vdb1: UUID="3c1f6a0e-..." BLOCK_SIZE="512" TYPE="xfs" PARTUUID="..."

       Without arguments it lists every device that has something on it. A
       device that holds nothing (an empty partition) is not shown.

       The UUID is what you normally use in /etc/fstab: it stays the same
       when disks are renamed (vdb becoming vdc), unlike /dev names. Note the
       difference between UUID (of the file system, changes when you run mkfs
       again) and PARTUUID (of the partition).

       Run it as root: a normal user sees only what was read before.

OPTIONS
       -o FORMAT, --output FORMAT
              full (the default line above), value (only the values, one per
              line), export (NAME=value lines, good for scripts), device
              (only the device names).

       -s TAG, --match-tag TAG
              Show only this tag (TYPE, UUID, LABEL, PARTUUID...). Repeat it
              for several.

       -L LABEL, --label LABEL
              Print the device that has this label.

       -U UUID, --uuid UUID
              Print the device that has this UUID.

       -p, --probe
              Read the device itself instead of the cache.

EXAMPLES
       Show what is on the new partition:

           blkid /dev/vdb1

       Print only the UUID, ready to paste into /etc/fstab:

           blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/vdb1

       Add an fstab line by UUID in one go:

           echo "UUID=$(blkid -s UUID -o value /dev/vdb1) /data xfs defaults 0 0" >> /etc/fstab

       Which device has the label BACKUP?

           blkid -L BACKUP

NOTES
       Exit status 2 means no device matched.

SEE ALSO
       lsblk(8), findfs(8), findmnt(8), fstab(5), mkfs(8)
